Dude, that’s a fascinating connection because you’re identifying something that happens across biology, cognition, technology, and even civilization:

An organism can extend its internal functions into the external world, transform something outside itself, and then selectively reintegrate the results.

And digestion may be one of the best biological analogies for understanding intelligence.

1. Digestion doesn’t have to happen inside the body

Spiders — external chemical digestion

Many spiders introduce digestive fluids into prey, breaking down tissues before ingesting liquefied nutrients. They don’t literally inject their stomachs.

Sea stars — externalized stomach

Some sea stars actually push their stomachs outside their bodies and digest prey externally before withdrawing them.

Fungi — environmental digestion

Fungi secrete enzymes into their surroundings, decompose complex organic matter, and absorb the resulting nutrients.

Leafcutter ants — cultivated digestion

Ants cultivate fungi that convert harvested plant material into food they can use. Their colony effectively incorporates another organism into its nutritional system.

There’s a common architecture underlying these remarkably different strategies:

Externalize a function → Transform the environment → Recover useful products → Integrate them into the organism.

2. Now apply that to human cognition

Imagine the human mind not as an isolated processor but as an organism capable of pushing parts of its information processing into its environment.

We already do this constantly.

* Language: We externalize thoughts into words so they can be examined, rearranged, challenged, and returned to us.
* Writing: We store information outside the nervous system, allowing thought to survive limitations of working memory.
* Mathematics: We manipulate external symbols to solve problems we couldn’t reliably calculate mentally.
* Other people: We distribute observation, interpretation, memory, and problem-solving across a social network.
* AI: We delegate portions of analysis, synthesis, retrieval, and creative exploration to computational systems, then evaluate and integrate their outputs.

Notice that these aren’t all merely storage devices.

They’re environments in which thought can be transformed.

A person can write down an idea, manipulate its components, discover an implication they couldn’t initially see, and internalize a transformed understanding.

That’s remarkably similar, at an abstract functional level, to externally processing material before absorbing it.

3. Cognitive digestion as an actual operator

The external cognition cycle

Encounter

New information, experience, or problem

Externalize

Speech, writing, symbols, tools, AI

Transform

Decompose, recombine, simulate, compare

Selectively integrate

Test, retain, reject, or revise

Act and learn

New behavior changes the next encounter

The cycle can repeat at individual, group, or civilizational scales.

The part I find particularly interesting is selective absorption.

Your stomach doesn’t simply preserve whatever you swallow in its original form. It breaks things apart and absorbs usable products, while excluding or eliminating much else.

Similarly, good thinking requires more than accepting whatever a book, another person, or an AI produces. It requires breaking claims apart, testing relationships, extracting useful structures, and rejecting what doesn’t survive examination.

Without that final discrimination, external cognition becomes dependence rather than increased capability.

4. Humanity has externalized both digestion and intelligence

Here’s another connection to your earlier ideas about energy and evolution.

Cooking is arguably one of humanity’s earliest forms of external metabolic processing.

Heat transforms food before it enters the digestive system. Cooking can make some nutrients more accessible and reduce the physical and biological work required to process food.

Agriculture then reorganized entire ecosystems around our nutritional requirements.

Language, writing, institutions, and computers did something analogous for information processing.

And there is a further level:

A civilization doesn’t simply think with individual human brains. It uses libraries, markets, scientific institutions, computer networks, and shared cultural practices to perform cognitive operations no individual could execute alone.

Those external processes consume real energy and resources, and they can introduce errors. But they allow cognitive work to extend beyond the capacities of a single nervous system.

5. The Pantheon connection: Exosomatic Metabolism

I’d call this broader operator Exosomatic Metabolism, borrowing exosomatic to mean outside the body.

It could have three interconnected expressions:

Domain	External transformation	What returns
Biological	Digestion, symbiosis, food preparation	Usable nutrients
Cognitive	Language, diagrams, computation, AI	Usable representations and knowledge
Civilizational	Institutions, infrastructure, collective research	Coordinated capabilities and accumulated knowledge

These are functional analogies, not identical biological processes.

But the same design questions recur: Where is the boundary? Which functions can move across it? What resources power the external system? What is returned? How does the organism distinguish useful material from harmful or misleading material?

And this is where it connects to your biological semantics work.

An organism’s functional boundary doesn’t always coincide with its skin.

A fungus extends chemical processing into its substrate. An ant colony depends on a cultivated fungal partner. A human extends memory into a notebook, then reasoning into a conversation or computational model.

The organism remains biologically distinct from those external systems, but some of its capabilities depend on their interaction.

And now consider the evolutionary implication:

What if one of the major routes to greater intelligence isn’t building an ever-larger brain, but becoming increasingly capable of organizing intelligence outside the brain?

That wouldn’t eliminate the importance of internal cognition. It would change the evolutionary problem from how much can one brain compute? to how effectively can an organism build, coordinate, and learn from external systems?

Which brings us back to your octopuses, ants, elephants, and alternative intelligences.

Different body plans might support radically different ways of externalizing cognition.

And in some evolutionary pathways, the most intelligent system might not be an individual creature at all, but the sustained relationship between creatures and the environments they’ve learned to transform.